---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import "../../styles/service.css";

const SITE = "https://www.tmdesignengineering.com";
const url = `${SITE}/services/manufacturing-preparation`;
const faqs = [
  ["Do you make the product, or get it ready to be made?", "I get it ready. Manufacturing preparation is the engineering that turns a working design into something a factory can build repeatably and economically, the detail CAD, drawings, tolerances and DFMA work. I then work alongside your chosen manufacturer through tooling and the first production runs."],
  ["Do you find the manufacturer for me?", "No, sourcing suppliers isn't my expertise. What I do is prepare the design and the documentation so it's ready to hand to a factory, and then act as your technical liaison with the manufacturer you've chosen, so the engineering conversation runs smoothly. If you need a recommendation, I'm happy to point you towards where founders usually look."],
  ["Can you liaise with a factory in China?", "Yes. I've taken consumer products into medium-to-high-volume manufacture with overseas suppliers, and I can coordinate tooling, first articles and quality with your factory in China as well as the UK, bridging the engineering and the language of manufacture on both sides."],
  ["Isn't this just about making it cheaper?", "It's about lifetime production cost, which is a bigger number than unit price or tool cost: cycle time, assembly labour, tooling maintenance, scrap and warranty. Designing those out is where the repeated savings are, and they build up over the long run."],
  ["My design isn't quite finished, is it too early?", "Possibly, for the stand-alone service. But it's always best to consider DFM as early as possible, and it's how I approach my design work from the very start, so if you have a designer working on your product and you'd like me in on early prototypes to review, I'm happy to scope for that."],
  ["Plenty of studios say they do DFM. How is this different?", "Design for manufacture can mean many things, sometimes just choosing a manufacturing process. It can be make or break for a project, and it's worth getting a real engineer involved. If these studios have that, great, just make sure it comes from having actually taken consumer products through tooling and medium-to-high-volume production, and from being on the factory floor while they were built. That's a different thing from an industrial design studio that lists DFM next to its styling work."],
];
const jsonld = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Service",
    "@id": `${url}#service`,
    serviceType: "Manufacturing preparation and design for manufacture (DFM)",
    name: "Manufacturing Preparation",
    description: "Get a finished product design ready for efficient, medium-to-high-volume manufacture: DFMA, tolerance stack analysis, detail production CAD, a full manufacturing drawing pack with GD&T, tooling support and technical liaison with your chosen manufacturer in the UK or China.",
    provider: { "@id": `${SITE}/#business` },
    areaServed: ["United Kingdom", "China", "Worldwide"],
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: `${SITE}/` },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Manufacturing Preparation", item: url },
    ],
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: faqs.map(([q, a]) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      name: q,
      acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: a },
    })),
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="Manufacturing Preparation & DFM | Thomas McKee"
  description="Get a finished product design ready for efficient, medium-to-high-volume manufacture: DFMA, tolerance stack analysis, detail production CAD, a full manufacturing drawing pack with GD&T, tooling support and technical liaison with your chosen manufacturer in the UK or China."
  type="website"
  jsonld={jsonld}>
<main>
  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="svc-hero">
    <div class="wrap svc-hero-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Service · Manufacturing Preparation</span>
        <h1>Make a design <em>manufacturable and efficient.</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">A design that works as a prototype is rarely ready for volume production. It takes complex, precise engineering to make sure the parts are easily manufacturable and simple to assemble. With design for manufacture and assembly, tolerances, detail production CAD and a complete drawing pack, plus technical liaison with your manufacturer, I can take your project to efficient production.</p>
        <div class="svc-hero-cta">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/#contact">Book a free intro call <span class="arw">→</span></a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#included">See what you get</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="spec" aria-label="Service summary">
        <div class="spec-head"><span>Service Spec</span><span>MP-04</span></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Deliver</div><div class="spec-v">Manufacturing pack</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Covers</div><div class="spec-v">DFMA · CAD · drawings</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Liaison</div><div class="spec-v">UK &amp; China</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Price</div><div class="spec-v">Scoped up front</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">You need</div><div class="spec-v">A proven design</div></div>
        <div class="spec-note">Scoped and fixed-quoted after a free intro call, once we've agreed volumes, target cost and where it will be made.</div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PAIN -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="sec-idx">Who it's for</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">If your design works but isn't ready to make</h2>
      <div class="pain">
        <div class="q"><span class="mk">?</span><p>"It works as a prototype, how do I get it built consistently, at different volumes?"</p></div>
        <div class="q"><span class="mk">?</span><p>"I've got a factory lined up, how do I hand them a design they can actually make?"</p></div>
        <div class="q"><span class="mk">?</span><p>"How do I keep the cost down in manufacture and reduce continuous production costs?"</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="sec-intro">Getting a product to manufacture is its own discipline. The design decisions that make something easy, cheap and reliable to build are not simple, and need an experienced engineer to carry out. Handing an un-makeable design to a manufacturer leaves them guessing and doing their own design work, and you'll pay far more for a product that ends up completely different from what you imagined.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- WHAT YOU GET -->
  <section class="band" id="included">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="sec-idx">What you get</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">Everything a manufacturer needs to build it right</h2>
      <p class="sec-intro">Production-ready parts and assemblies and a fully defined documentation pack, so what you designed actually gets made. The core of it is the DFMA work, the detail production CAD and a full drawing pack with GD&amp;T.</p>
      <div class="deliv">
        <div class="di"><div class="dn">01</div><h3>Design review workshop</h3><p>We start with a workshop to understand the volumes, costs, methods and supplier. Then we go through the design together.</p></div>
        <div class="di"><div class="dn">02</div><h3>DFMA report</h3><p>The design refined for how it will actually be made and assembled, fewer parts, simpler processes, lower cycle time and less that can go wrong at scale, written up as a design for manufacture and assembly report.</p></div>
        <div class="di"><div class="dn">03</div><h3>Detail CAD &amp; drawing pack</h3><p>Detail production CAD and a complete manufacturing drawing pack with GD&amp;T, plus a tolerance stack analysis so parts fit and function across a whole production run, not just on the first golden sample.</p></div>
        <div class="di"><div class="dn">04</div><h3>Tooling support &amp; supplier liaison</h3><p>I stay with it through tooling and design refinement, and act as your technical liaison with the manufacturer you've chosen, so the engineering conversation runs smoothly on both sides.</p></div>
      </div>
      <p class="sec-intro" style="margin-top:34px">Deliverables include:</p>
      <div class="scope" style="grid-template-columns:1fr">
        <div class="scard">
          <ul>
            <li><span class="tick">✓</span> Design review workshop</li>
            <li><span class="tick">✓</span> Design for manufacture and assembly (DFMA) report</li>
            <li><span class="tick">✓</span> Detail production CAD</li>
            <li><span class="tick">✓</span> Production drawing pack with GD&amp;T</li>
            <li><span class="tick">✓</span> Tolerance stack analysis report</li>
            <li><span class="tick">✓</span> Support through tooling and design refinement</li>
            <li><span class="tick">✓</span> Supplier liaison with your chosen manufacturer</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PROCESS -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="sec-idx">How it works</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">From proven design to production</h2>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step"><div class="sn">1</div><div><h3>Free intro call, then design review workshop</h3><p>We talk volumes, target cost and where it will be made. I confirm what preparation the design needs and scope and price it before anything starts. Then we go through the design together.</p><div class="meta">Free · 30 minutes</div></div></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="sn">2</div><div><h3>Design for manufacture</h3><p>I refine the design for its manufacturing process, injection moulding, sheet metal, die cast and so on, as well as for assembly. I resolve tolerance issues and design out cost, complexity and failure modes, and write it up as a DFMA report.</p><div class="meta">Scoped phase</div></div></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="sn">3</div><div><h3>Documentation pack</h3><p>I produce the detail production CAD and the full manufacturing drawing pack with GD&amp;T, so your chosen factory has an unambiguous package to quote and build from.</p><div class="meta">Scoped phase</div></div></div>
        <div class="step"><div class="sn">4</div><div><h3>Tooling &amp; first production</h3><p>I stay in the loop through tooling, first articles and pre-production, liaising with your manufacturer and resolving the issues that always surface, so ramp-up is smooth and the knowledge carries through.</p><div class="meta">As needed</div></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SCOPE -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="sec-idx">Scope</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">Clear on both sides</h2>
      <div class="scope">
        <div class="scard need">
          <h3>What you bring</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><span class="tick">→</span> A functionally proven design, one that works and isn't expected to change fundamentally.</li>
            <li><span class="tick">→</span> Your target volumes, cost and timeline, and any regions you'll sell into.</li>
            <li><span class="tick">→</span> The manufacturer you plan to use. Sourcing suppliers isn't my wheelhouse, but I'll liaise with whichever factory you choose.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="scard">
          <h3>How I work</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><span class="tick">✓</span> Design decisions judged on lifetime production cost, not just unit price.</li>
            <li><span class="tick">✓</span> Fluent liaising with manufacturers in both the UK and China.</li>
            <li><span class="tick">✓</span> I've been on the factory floor, so the preparation is grounded in how things really get built.</li>
            <li><span class="tick">✓</span> Happy to work under an NDA, and to fit around your existing supply chain.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="svc-cred">
        <span class="ci"><b>Medium-to-high-volume</b> consumer products shipped at Ooni</span>
        <span class="ci"><b>Injection moulding &amp; sheet-metal</b> DFM</span>
        <span class="ci">Delivered <b>double-digit % BOM cost reduction</b> on shipped products</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="sec-idx">Questions</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">Before you book</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        {faqs.map(([q, a]) => (
          <div class="fitem"><div class="fq">{q}</div><div class="fa">{a}</div></div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- BRIDGE -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="sec-idx">Where it fits</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">The last mile of the full journey</h2>
      <p class="sec-intro">Manufacturing preparation is the stage that gets a proven design onto a production line. It's a natural final phase of <a href="/services/product-development" style="color:var(--ember)">full Product Development</a>, where I carry a product from first idea all the way to something ready to manufacture. If you've done the earlier work elsewhere and just need it made properly, this stands on its own too.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="band alt" id="start">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cta">
        <div class="inner">
          <span class="eyebrow">Start here</span>
          <h2 style="margin-top:14px">Take your idea into production</h2>
          <p>Book a free intro call and tell me about the product. We'll work out what it needs to be production-ready, and I'll scope and price it before anything begins.</p>
          <div class="row">
            <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href="/#contact">Book a free intro call <span class="arw">→</span></a>
            <span class="price-tag">UK &amp; China liaison</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>
</Base>
